.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
.TH PKGFILE 5 "" "imai #VERSION#" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
Pkgfile \- trusted Bash recipe for a CRUX software package
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR pkgmk (8)
reads Pkgfile from the port directory. The recipe describes source files and
how to stage a package. This is the CRUX Pkgfile interface, not an Arch
PKGBUILD interface or a replacement POSIX recipe language.
.PP
The C executable launches a persistent Bash controller. Defaults are initialized,
command-line options are parsed, then Pkgfile and the selected pkgmk.conf are
sourced in that order. Recipe or configuration assignments can override
command-line settings. Bash arrays, functions and configuration overrides
remain available in the same controller context. The build function runs in
a subshell with Bash errexit and command tracing enabled; changes made there
do not propagate back into the controller. Bash's normal exceptions to errexit
apply; it is not a substitute for explicit error handling where required.
.SH DEFINITIONS
.TP
.B name
Package name, conventionally lowercase and equal to the port directory name.
.TP
.B version
Upstream version string.
.TP
.B release
Port packaging revision, normally starting at 1.
.TP
.B source
Bash array of remote URLs and local source filenames. Remote sources are
stored in PKGMK_SOURCE_DIR. Recognized archives are extracted with bsdtar;
other files are copied into SRC.
.TP
.B renames
Optional Bash array aligned with source. Each element gives a downloaded
source's local name, or SKIP to retain its original name. Avoid relying on
renamed archive filenames in build(): extraction contents do not change.
.TP
.B build()
Required Bash function that compiles the sources and stages the installed
files below PKG. It must not install directly into the running system.
.SH BUILD VARIABLES
.TP
.B SRC
Source working directory. Use it to access unpacked sources and local files.
.TP
.B PKG
Staging root for package contents. Use quoted expansions when passing it to
commands, for example make DESTDIR="$PKG" install.
.PP
Recipes can inspect configuration variables when build() executes. Because
configuration is sourced after Pkgfile, a top-level recipe expression does not
yet see later configuration assignments. Recipes and configuration can override
controller functions, but unnecessary private variable names risk collisions
with future pkgmk internals.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
# Description: Example library
# URL: https://example.invalid/library
# Maintainer: Port maintainer
# Depends on: otherlib

name=examplelib
version=1.2.3
release=1
source=(https://example.invalid/$name-$version.tar.gz
        fix.patch)

build() {
        cd "$name-$version"
        patch -p1 < "$SRC/fix.patch"
        ./configure --prefix=/usr
        make
        make DESTDIR="$PKG" install
}
.fi
.PP
Header comments are conventions consumed by other CRUX ports tools. pkgmk
does not resolve or install dependencies from the Depends on field.
For distribution policy, consult the current CRUX port guidelines rather than
treating policy recommendations as parser requirements.
.SH SAFETY
The entire recipe and executable configuration are trusted code, including
top-level statements before build(). They run with the caller's privileges;
root builds are supported and are not sandboxed. Source extraction and build
tools can also execute code. Neither PKG staging nor signatures turn an
untrusted recipe into safe input.
.PP
A footprint records expected archive layout and metadata, not package
authenticity or complete reproducibility. Binary packages installed by pkgadd
remain unsigned local input under the pkgutils trust model.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I Pkgfile
Package recipe in the port directory.
.TP
.I .footprint
Expected package footprint.
.TP
.I .md5sum
Legacy source checksums.
.TP
.I .signature
Port signature and SHA256 sums.
.TP
.I /etc/pkgmk.conf
Executable build configuration.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pkgmk.conf (5),
.BR pkgmk (8),
.BR pkgadd (8),
.BR bash (1)
.PP
CRUX port guidelines: https://crux.nu/Main/PortGuidelines
